    {
      strand: "4. RELIGION AND CONTEMPORARY ISSUES",
      subStrands: [
        {
          name: "1. RELIGION AND THE ENVIRONMENT",
          learningOutcomes: [
            { code: "1.4.1.LO.1", text: "Explain human activities that affect the environment and describe how environmental values in the teachings of Christianity align with AIR, Science and Technology for the care of the environment." }
          ],
          skills21stCentury: "Critical Thinking and Problem-Solving\n\u2022 Learners critically evaluate the causes and effects of human activities on the environment.\n\u2022 They analyse how religious beliefs and scientific methods provide complementary ethical and practical responses to environmental degradation.\n\u2022 Through dialogue and inquiry, learners propose solutions that integrate Christian, AIR, and scientific values.\n\nCreativity\n\u2022 Learners creatively express religious environmental values through posters, poetry, songs, and digital media.\n\u2022 Through the \"Creation Care is Christian Duty\" campaign, learners explore imaginative ways of advocating sustainable behaviours.\n\u2022 Concept mapping and digital word clouds allow learners to visually synthesise diverse values and principles.\n\nCollaboration\n\u2022 Group research, discussions, and planning of advocacy initiatives foster effective teamwork across diverse backgrounds.\n\u2022 Learners work in mixed-gender and multi-faith groups, modelling inclusive, collaborative problem-solving for environmental care.\n\nCommunication\n\u2022 Learners practise verbal, written, and digital communication through class debates, interviews, digital projects, and environmental advocacy.\n\u2022 Structured class discussions help learners articulate religious, scientific, and ethical perspectives on the environment.\n\nLifelong Learning\n\u2022 Learners develop an inquiry mindset as they investigate faith-based and scientific approaches to sustainability.\n\u2022 Exposure to religious and environmental institutions encourages learners to pursue continual learning and action on global challenges.\n\nGlobal and Local (Glocal) Citizenship\n\u2022 Learners relate global environmental issues to local religious practices, taboos, and traditions.\n\u2022 Comparative analysis of Christian and AIR teachings fosters respect for indigenous knowledge and global stewardship ethics.\n\nNormative Competency\n\u2022 Learners reflect on their values in light of environmental stewardship in both Christianity and AIR.\n\u2022 They question personal attitudes and examine moral obligations toward future generations and ecological justice.\n\nSystems Thinking\n\u2022 Learners recognise how ecological, religious, and scientific systems interact.\n\u2022 Mapping exercises help learners understand how various actors (religious groups, science, traditional practices) contribute to or mitigate environmental harm.\n\nAnticipatory Competency\n\u2022 Learners consider long-term impacts of environmental neglect and develop future-oriented strategies for sustainability.\n\u2022 Scenario-based activities and reflections help learners assess environmental risks and ethical duties.\n\nStrategic Competency\n\u2022 Learners develop action plans that combine environmental theology, indigenous traditions, and science to address environmental issues in their communities.\n\u2022 They propose interfaith and interdisciplinary collaboration strategies for climate justice and ecological health.",
          gesiSelValues: "GESI:\n\u2022 Group tasks intentionally involve mixed gender and ability groups, fostering equitable learning opportunities.\n\u2022 Learners examine how environmental issues affect marginalised groups (e.g., women and rural dwellers) and propose inclusive solutions.\n\u2022 Through role models (e.g., female EPA officers or church environmental leaders), learners challenge stereotypes in science and faith-based leadership.\n\u2022 Learners value contributions of minority religious traditions and indigenous knowledge systems in addressing environmental concerns.\n\nSEL:\nSelf-Awareness\nLearners reflect on personal consumption habits and their environmental impact, aligning behaviour with religious and ethical values.\n\nSocial Awareness\n\u2022 Learners develop empathy for communities affected by environmental harm and appreciate cultural wisdom in AIR.\n\u2022 Dialogue about taboos, sacred sites, and global climate challenges fosters a deeper understanding of shared responsibility.\n\nRelationship Skills\n\u2022 Collaborative tasks and respectful debates teach learners to engage with different perspectives and build consensus.\n\u2022 Through peer teaching and shared research, learners develop trust and communication within diverse teams.\n\nResponsible Decision-Making\n\u2022 Learners use religious teachings, traditional values, and scientific evidence to guide environmentally sound decision-making.\n\u2022 Project-based learning tasks reinforce the importance of applying values to everyday actions and policies.\n\nNational Core Values\nRespect & Diversity: Learners recognise the legitimacy of both Christian and indigenous approaches to environmental protection.\nTruthfulness & Honesty: Learners report accurate research findings and engage in ethical reflection.\nTolerance & Social Cohesion: Dialogue across religious and scientific perspectives fosters mutual understanding and social harmony.\nLoyalty & Civic Responsibility: Learners see themselves as stewards of Ghana's natural resources, linking religious care to national service.\nJustice: Learners advocate for the protection of ecosystems and people, especially the vulnerable, guided by the principle of ecological justice rooted in both faith and science.",
          contentStandards: [
            {
              code: "1.4.1.CS.1",
              title: "Demonstrate an understanding of the environmental values in the teachings of Christianity and show how they can be combined with AIR and Science to address environmental problems.",
              indicators: [
                {
                  code: "1.4.1.LI.1",
                  text: "Identify human activities that affect the environment.\n\nEnquiry Route\n1. Human activities that negatively affect the environment (e.g., illegal mining, deforestation, plastic waste disposal, air pollution).\n2. Human practices that promote environmental sustainability (e.g., tree planting, recycling, proper waste management).\n3. The consequences of environmentally harmful practices for health, biodiversity, and climate.",
                  exemplar: "Experiential Learning: Learners are taken on a guided school compound or community walk to observe environmental degradation (e.g., littering, erosion, smoke from burning waste, galamsey sites). They return to reflect on what they observed and how such activities harm the environment. This hands-on engagement promotes environmental awareness and personal connection.\n\nGroup Work/Collaborative Learning: Groups are assigned to research specific human activities (e.g., illegal mining, bush burning, plastic pollution). Each group explains the activity, its environmental effects, and how it can be controlled. Presentations are followed by peer questions and feedback. This promotes teamwork and active research skills.\n\nConcept Mapping (Structuring Talk for Learning): Learners draw a concept map linking specific human activities to their environmental impacts (e.g., deforestation \u2192 loss of rainfall \u2192 crop failure). This visual representation enhances understanding of cause-and-effect relationships and supports critical analysis.\n\nTalk for Learning (Guided Discussion with Problem Solving): Start a class discussion with the question: \"What everyday activities in our homes or schools affect the environment?\" Learners contribute examples and suggest solutions. This dialogue helps connect personal responsibility to broader ecological concerns and fosters moral reflection.",
                  assessment: { code: "1.4.1.AS.1", levels: ["Level 1 Recall","Level 2 Skills of conceptual understanding","Level 3 Strategic reasoning","Level 4 Extended critical thinking and reasoning"] }
                },
                {
                  code: "1.4.1.LI.2",
                  text: "Identify the environmental values in the teachings of Christianity\n\nEnquiry Route\n1. Biblical teachings that promote care for the environment (e.g., stewardship, dominion, creation care) - Gen. 2:15; Gen. 1: 29-30; Ps. 24: 1; 50: 9-12 and Job 4: 11\n2. Christian responsibility to protect and sustain God\u2019s creation.\n3. How Christian values such as respect for life, justice, and compassion apply to environmental issues.\n4. The role of the Church and believers in promoting environmental sustainability in Ghana",
                  exemplar: "Talk for Learning (Scripture-Based Discussion): Initiate a discussion on biblical texts such as Genesis 2:15 (\"The Lord God took the man and put him in the Garden of Eden to work it and take care of it\") and Psalm 24:1 (\"The earth is the Lord's\"). Learners reflect on how these verses promote environmental care. This develops scriptural interpretation and value-based reasoning.\n\nConcept Mapping (Structuring Talk for Learning): Learners create a map linking Christian values (e.g., stewardship, gratitude, responsibility) to environmental practices (e.g., tree planting, reducing pollution, caring for animals). This helps learners visualise how faith translates into action and reinforces ethical reasoning.\n\nInquiry-Based Learning: Assign learners to explore how different Christian groups in Ghana (e.g., Catholic Diocese, Presbyterian Church, SDA, Pentecostal churches) are involved in environmental projects such as sanitation, afforestation, or climate advocacy. Learners report findings and reflect on how Christian teachings inspire these initiatives. This fosters independent inquiry and application of knowledge.\n\nProject-Based Learning: Learners design a mini-campaign titled \"Creation Care is Christian Duty\"\u2014developing posters, songs, or poems that communicate Christian environmental values to peers or the school community. This promotes creativity, moral education, and civic engagement from a faith-based perspective.\n\nDigital pedagogy: Learners use technological tools to design \"word clouds\" on the key environmental values propounded by Christianity and AIR in Ghana.",
                  assessment: { code: "1.4.1.AS.2", levels: ["Level 1 Recall","Level 2 Skills of conceptual understanding","Level 3 Strategic reasoning","Level 4 Extended critical thinking and reasoning"] }
                },
                {
                  code: "1.4.1.LI.3",
                  text: "Comparatively analyse how Christian values align with those of AIR on environmental protection\n\nEnquiry Route:\n1. Key environmental values in both Christianity and AIR (e.g., stewardship, respect for creation, sacredness of nature).\n2. Similarities and differences in the religious teachings and practices of Christianity and AIR on nature and the environment.\n3. Potential of interfaith collaboration for sustainable environmental care in Ghana.",
                  exemplar: "Talk for Learning (Comparative Dialogue): Begin with the question: \"What do Christianity and African Indigenous Religion say about nature?\" Learners discuss values such as stewardship (Christianity) and sacred groves/taboos (AIR). Encourage learners to identify overlaps and reflect on how these beliefs promote sustainability. This promotes comparative thinking and interreligious respect.\n\nConcept Mapping (Structuring Talk for Learning): Learners develop a two-column concept map comparing Christian and AIR environmental values (e.g., Christianity: God as Creator \u2192 Stewardship, AIR: Ancestors protect forests \u2192 Respect for sacred sites). Arrows may show where values align or diverge. This visual method supports synthesis and clarity.\n\nGroup Work/Collaborative Learning: Assign groups to focus on a specific environmental theme (e.g., land use, water sources, animal care). Each group investigates how both Christianity and AIR approach this issue, then presents their comparative analysis. This encourages cooperative inquiry and depth of understanding.\n\nDiamond Nine Activity: Provide cards with nine shared or contrasting values (e.g., respect for life, sacredness of creation, human-nature harmony, punishment for environmental harm). Learners rank them in order of significance for environmental protection in the Ghanaian context. This supports prioritisation, debate, and consensus-building.",
                  assessment: { code: "1.4.1.AS.3", levels: ["Level 1 Recall","Level 2 Skills of conceptual understanding","Level 3 Strategic reasoning","Level 4 Extended critical thinking and reasoning"] }
                },
                {
                  code: "1.4.1.LI.4",
                  text: "Examine how Christian values can complement science and technology for the protection of the environment.\n\nEnquiry Route\n1. Shortcomings of science in solving all environmental problems (e.g., focus on concepts and theories on environment and less on attitudinal change).\n2. Environmental problems raise questions of theory, ethics and philosophy. Using science alone without complementary role of religion cannot solve the problem.\n3. Christianity\u2019s contribution to environmental protection\n    a. Biblical imperative in Deut. 20:19-20 has influence the practice of Catholics\u2019 Arbor Week used to plant trees,\n    b. Church of Pentecost\u2019s 1million tree planting project in 2021,\n    c. Adventist\u2019 NGO- ADRA and other Christian denominations undertaking varied environmental protection programmes",
                  exemplar: "Resource Person: Learners examine the presentation of a science or geography teacher/an official from EPA, (preferably a female) on how to sustain the environment.\n\nExperiential Learning: Learners watch a video or PowerPoint slides on scientific knowledge and technological tools, used to protect or sustain the environment for class discussion.\n\nBuild on what others say: Learners discuss shortcomings of science in solving environmental problems.\n\nDigital pedagogy: Using online sources, learners search for news report on the environmental protection programmes undertaken by religious institutions.\n\nCollaborative learning: In groups of mixed gender/ability/religion, learners brainstorm on how religion, science and technology can complement each other in solving environmental problems.",
                  assessment: { code: "1.4.1.AS.4", levels: ["Level 1 Recall","Level 2 Skills of conceptual understanding","Level 3 Strategic reasoning","Level 4 Extended critical thinking and reasoning"] }
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          teachingResources: "Resource persons from Christianity and AIR; Video or pictures; The Bible; Costume for drama; Posters"
        }
      ]
    }
